const ROWS=[
 ['character',null,'What is a foil in a longer work?','A character whose contrasting traits highlight those of another character.',['A minor character who never speaks.','A character who disguises himself as someone else.'],'A foil is set beside another character so that the differences sharpen both.'],
 ['character','foil','How does Dev work as a foil to Mara?','His carefree ease sets off her careful orderliness.',['He is just as careful, so they match.','He narrates the story about her.'],'The two are contrasted: she counts and records, he laughs and trusts luck.'],
 ['character',null,'What is a dynamic character?','A character who changes in a significant way over the course of the work.',['A character who appears in every scene.','A character who is always in motion.'],'Dynamic means the character is altered, in outlook or behavior, by events.'],
 ['character',null,'What is a static character?','A character who stays essentially the same throughout the work.',['A character who never speaks.','A character who dies early in the plot.'],'A static character may matter a great deal without undergoing change.'],
 ['character','dyn','What kind of character is Anselm?','A dynamic character, because his generosity replaces his stinginess.',['A static character, because he lives in one place.','A foil, because he is the narrator.'],'He moves from refusing even a lantern to handing lanterns to strangers.'],
 ['character','stat','What kind of character is Hobb?','A static character, because he does not change.',['A dynamic character, because the town acts on him.','A narrator, because he answers every request.'],'The passage stresses that nothing moves him from first page to last.'],
 ['character',null,'In a play or novel, who is the protagonist?','The central character whose goals and struggles drive the plot.',['The character who tells the audience what to feel.','The character who has the most lines.'],'The protagonist is the main focus of the conflict, not necessarily the most talkative.'],
 ['character',null,'What is an antagonist?','A character or force that opposes the protagonist.',['A character who is always evil.','A character who is nameless.'],'An antagonist may be a person, a group or a condition such as a society; it need not be a villain.'],
 ['character',null,'What is a flat character?','A character with few traits who does not develop much.',['A character who is lying down on stage.','A character with many contradictory traits.'],'Flat characters are simple and predictable; round characters are complex.'],
 ['character',null,'What makes a character round?','Complex, sometimes contradictory traits, and the ability to surprise the reader.',['Having a round face described in detail.','Appearing in only one scene.'],'Round characters have depth that a short label cannot capture.'],
 ['character',null,'What is indirect characterization?','Showing character through speech, actions, thoughts and the reactions of others.',['A narrator telling us plainly what a character is like.','Describing only a character’s clothes.'],'Direct characterization states traits; indirect characterization lets the reader infer them.'],
 ['character','indirect','How does this passage characterize Tess?','Indirectly, through her actions, which show anger she does not state.',['Directly, by telling the reader she is calm.','Through another character’s opinion of her.'],'She never says she is angry; folding the letter and washing the cup three times show it.'],
 ['character',null,'In Shakespeare’s Hamlet, how does Laertes work as a foil to Hamlet?','When his father is killed, he acts quickly to avenge him, while Hamlet delays.',['Like Hamlet, he delays for most of the play.','He is the king whom Hamlet must obey.'],'Laertes’s swift action sets off Hamlet’s hesitation about avenging his own father.'],
 ['character',null,'In Hamlet, Fortinbras, a prince of Norway, leads an army to win a small piece of land for honor. What does he do as a foil?','His decisive action contrasts with Hamlet’s hesitation.',['His cruelty matches the cruelty of Claudius.','His love story contrasts with Ophelia’s.'],'Hamlet himself reflects on Fortinbras’s readiness to act while he keeps delaying.'],
 ['character',null,'In Ibsen’s A Doll’s House (1879), what decision shows how far Nora has changed by the end?','She leaves her husband and children to find out who she is.',['She confesses to Krogstad and stays.','She forgives Torvald and returns to the party.'],'Nora’s departure at the end is the play’s most famous act of change.'],
 ['character',null,'In A Doll’s House, how does Mrs. Linde work as a foil to Nora?','She has worked and supported herself through hardship, unlike the sheltered Nora.',['She is as sheltered and carefree as Nora.','She is Nora’s rival in the Helmer household.'],'Christine Linde’s experience of work and loss highlights Nora’s protected life.'],
 ['character',null,'In Wilde’s The Importance of Being Earnest (1895), Jack invents a brother named Ernest and Algernon invents an invalid friend, Bunbury. What does this show?','Both men keep up a made-up double life to escape duties, so each mirrors the other.',['Both men are devoted to the truth.','Both men are forced to lie by the law.'],'The parallel inventions make Jack and Algernon comic doubles who satirize Victorian propriety.'],
 ['character',null,'In Wharton’s Ethan Frome (1911), how does Mattie Silver work as a foil to Zeena?','Her youth and cheer contrast with Zeena’s sickly, complaining manner.',['Her wealth contrasts with Zeena’s poverty.','Her age matches Zeena’s, so they are twins.'],'Mattie’s warmth makes Zeena’s cold ill-temper plainer, and draws Ethan toward her.'],
 ['character',null,'In Brontë’s Jane Eyre (1847), St. John Rivers is often read as a foil to Rochester. Which contrast supports that reading?','He offers a marriage of duty without love, against Rochester’s passionate bond with Jane.',['He is rich and Rochester is poor.','He lives at Thornfield and Rochester at Moor House.'],'St. John’s cold sense of mission sets off the warmth and passion Jane finds with Rochester.'],
 ['setting',null,'What does setting include in a longer work?','The time and place of the action, and often the social conditions of the world.',['Only the name of the town.','Only the weather.'],'Setting covers when and where the story happens and the customs and class structure around it.'],
 ['setting',null,'What is mood?','The feeling or atmosphere a work creates for the reader.',['The narrator’s attitude toward the main character.','The central conflict of the plot.'],'Setting details are among the strongest makers of mood.'],
 ['setting','house','What mood do the details in this passage create?','Neglect and abandonment.',['Festive celebration.','Cheerful bustle.'],'Weeds, crooked shutters and unswept steps all suggest a place no one cares for.'],
 ['setting','house','Which detail does the most to create the mood of the passage?','“Weeds grew through the porch boards”',['“The house had a green door”','“faced east”'],'The green door and the east-facing side are neutral facts; the weeds in the boards point to decay.'],
 ['setting',null,'What is a symbol?','An object, person, place or action that stands for a larger idea.',['A figure of speech using like or as.','A plot summary placed at the start.'],'A symbol means more than itself, and a reading of it needs support from the text.'],
 ['setting','symbol','What might the unopened letters beside the stopped clock suggest?','Avoidance of news and a life that has stalled.',['A busy, welcoming household.','A mail strike in the town.'],'Unopened letters suggest she avoids what they say, and the stopped clock suggests time has halted for her.'],
 ['setting',null,'What is a motif?','A recurring image, phrase or idea that helps develop a theme.',['A single event that ends the plot.','A character’s given name.'],'A motif returns over the work and builds meaning through repetition.'],
 ['setting',null,'In Fitzgerald’s The Great Gatsby (1925), the green light at the end of Daisy’s dock is commonly read as a symbol of what?','Gatsby’s longing for Daisy and for the future he imagines with her.',['The money the Buchanans owe.','A warning of storms on Long Island Sound.'],'Readers commonly link the light to Gatsby’s hope; it is a reading, supported by how he gazes at it.'],
 ['setting',null,'In The Great Gatsby, the “valley of ashes” between West Egg and New York is commonly read as a sign of what?','The moral and social decay beneath the glitter of the wealthy.',['The simple happiness of country life.','The beauty of Gatsby’s gardens.'],'The gray dumping ground contrasts with the lavish parties and shows who pays for the rich’s pleasures.'],
 ['setting',null,'In The Great Gatsby, how are West Egg and East Egg contrasted?','West Egg holds the newly rich, like Gatsby, and East Egg the old-money families, like the Buchanans.',['West Egg is a city and East Egg a farm.','West Egg is poor and East Egg is empty.'],'The two places stand for new money and old money and the social gap between them.'],
 ['setting',null,'In Ethan Frome, the village is named Starkfield and the story is set in winter. What does the setting emphasize?','Cold, isolation and the trap that Ethan’s life has become.',['Wealth and leisure.','Summer festivals.'],'The bleak New England winter mirrors Ethan’s confinement.'],
 ['setting',null,'In A Doll’s House, all the action takes place in the Helmers’ living room. What does this setting do?','It keeps the focus inside the marriage, while outside pressures enter through visitors and letters.',['It shows the play has no real conflict.','It proves Nora is an outsider to the family.'],'A single domestic room makes the home the stage for the play’s conflict.'],
 ['setting',null,'In A Midsummer Night’s Dream, how do the court of Athens and the nearby forest contrast?','The court is ruled by law and order; in the forest, magic and desire upset reason.',['The court is magical and the forest is orderly.','Both places are ruled by the same laws.'],'The lovers flee the harsh law of Athens into a night of confusion and enchantment.'],
 ['setting','hamlet4','What does Marcellus’s remark suggest about Denmark?','The kingdom itself is morally corrupt, not just a place on a map.',['The castle is about to be repainted.','Denmark has lost a war.'],'The remark treats the state as diseased, hinting at the crime behind the ghost’s appearance.'],
 ['setting',null,'Jane Eyre moves from Gateshead to Lowood, Thornfield, Moor House and Ferndean. What does the sequence of settings do?','Each place marks a stage in Jane’s growth and a new test of her independence.',['It repeats the same events in a new town.','It shows she never leaves home.'],'The changes of place map the changes in Jane’s life and self-understanding.'],
 ['setting',null,'Frankenstein opens with letters from an explorer in the Arctic. What does that setting do?','It frames the story with ambition and isolation, far from ordinary society.',['It shows the book is a tourist guide.','It sets the whole novel in Geneva.'],'The ice and the ship set a stage for Victor’s tale of ambition and the limits of human reach.'],
 ['setting','mill','What does the silence suggest about the mill town?','The mill has stopped, and the town’s life depends on it.',['The shift has begun as usual.','The whole story has moved to another country.'],'The passage ties the whistle to the working day, so its absence means a halt.'],
 ['setting',null,'What does the social setting of a work include?','The customs, class structure and beliefs of the community in which it takes place.',['Only the time of day.','Only the weather in each scene.'],'Social setting is the world of manners and power that shapes the characters’ choices.'],
 ['structure',null,'What happens in the exposition of a longer work?','The author introduces the characters, setting and situation.',['The central conflict is decided.','The story ends.'],'Exposition gives the background a reader needs to follow what comes next.'],
 ['structure','expo','What does this opening do?','Introduces the setting and the main character and her situation.',['Resolves the central conflict.','Reveals a flashback.'],'The sentence names the place, the long isolation and Wren’s job, all background.'],
 ['structure',null,'What is the rising action?','The series of events and complications that build the conflict.',['The final scene.','The quiet ending after the climax.'],'Each complication adds pressure on the way to the climax.'],
 ['structure',null,'What is the climax of a plot?','The turning point of highest tension, where the central conflict is decided.',['The very first scene.','A summary of the work.'],'After the climax the outcome is no longer in doubt.'],
 ['structure',null,'What is the falling action?','The events after the climax that lead toward the end.',['The first complication.','The author’s biography.'],'The falling action ties up threads after the main turn.'],
 ['structure',null,'What is the resolution (or denouement) of a work?','The final outcome, in which the conflicts are settled or left settled enough to end.',['The opening setup.','The point of highest tension.'],'Denouement comes from a French word meaning to untie.'],
 ['structure',null,'What is a subplot?','A secondary storyline that runs alongside the main plot and often echoes or complicates it.',['A plot that has been shortened.','A summary of the main plot.'],'Subplots deepen the main action by contrast or parallel.'],
 ['structure',null,'In Hamlet, the story of Laertes and Polonius and the march of Fortinbras run alongside Hamlet’s own. What is their role?','They are subplots that parallel Hamlet’s duty to avenge a father and test it by comparison.',['They interrupt the play with no connection.','They are the central conflict of the play.'],'Each shows another young man answering a wrong or a call to action, and each sheds light on Hamlet.'],
 ['structure',null,'In A Midsummer Night’s Dream, a group of workmen rehearse and perform the story of Pyramus and Thisbe. What does this subplot do?','It adds comedy and echoes the lovers’ troubles, staged as farce.',['It replaces the main plot.','It makes the play a tragedy.'],'The craftsmen’s clumsy play at the wedding both mirrors and mocks the lovers’ story.'],
 ['structure',null,'What is a flashback?','A scene or passage that takes the reader back to an earlier time.',['A hint of what will happen later.','A speech by a character who is alone.'],'A flashback interrupts the present to show the past.'],
 ['structure','flash','What does the shift from the bridge scene to Tomas at nine do?','It brings in a memory that deepens the meaning of the present.',['It ends the story.','It shows Tomas speaking to the audience.'],'The memory of his father holding his coat colors the present moment on the bridge.'],
 ['structure',null,'What is foreshadowing?','Hints early in a work about what will happen later.',['A full account of past events.','A repeated line at the end of an act.'],'Foreshadowing prepares for an outcome without stating it.'],
 ['structure','fore','What does the pilot’s silence about the dark line in the west foreshadow?','A coming storm or danger on the crossing.',['A happy arrival.','A change of narrator.'],'He sees a warning sign and says nothing, which signals trouble ahead.'],
 ['structure','caesar3','What does the warning foreshadow in Julius Caesar?','The assassination of Caesar on the Ides of March.',['The marriage of Caesar.','Caesar’s retirement to the country.'],'Caesar is killed by conspirators in Act 3 on the day he is warned about.'],
 ['structure',null,'What does in medias res mean?','Beginning a story in the middle of the action.',['Beginning with the ending.','Beginning with a long speech by the author.'],'The Latin phrase means “into the middle of things.”'],
 ['structure',null,'In Julius Caesar, which event do readers often treat as the turning point of the plot?','The assassination of Caesar in Act 3.',['The battle of Philippi in Act 5.','Calpurnia’s dream in Act 2.'],'Everything before builds to the conspiracy and everything after follows from the murder; readers differ on exact labels.'],
 ['structure','hamlet2','What does Hamlet plan to do with the play?','Watch Claudius’s reaction to a performance of the murder to test his guilt.',['Persuade Ophelia to marry him.','Convince the court he is mad.'],'The play is a trap to see whether the king’s face betrays him.'],
 ['structure',null,'How does Wilde’s The Importance of Being Earnest end?','In a comic reversal: Jack learns he was named Ernest after all, and the couples are united.',['With the death of Lady Bracknell.','With Jack’s exile from England.'],'Wilde’s farce resolves in engagements and a joke on the name Ernest.'],
 ['structure',null,'Ethan Frome begins and ends with a narrator in the present, and tells the main events as a story from the past. What does this structure do?','It frames the tragedy in retrospect and lets the ending show what the years have cost.',['It removes the conflict from the story.','It makes the events happen in order from day one.'],'The frame tells us early that something terrible has happened, then shows how.'],
 ['structure',null,'Nick Carraway tells The Great Gatsby after the summer is over. How does this retrospective structure affect the telling?','He can hint at the ending and comment with hindsight.',['He cannot know how the story ends.','The story is told in real time.'],'Looking back, Nick can shape and foreshadow what he describes.'],
 ['structure',null,'In A Doll’s House, Torvald reads Krogstad’s letter and reacts with anger. Why is this often seen as the turning point?','It exposes Torvald’s selfishness, and Nora sees her marriage clearly for the first time.',['Nora learns that Krogstad is her father.','Torvald dies of shock.'],'His reaction ends Nora’s hope for a “wonderful” act of love and prompts her decision.'],
 ['structure',null,'Which part of the plot comes right after the climax?','The falling action.',['The exposition.','The rising action.'],'The usual order is exposition, rising action, climax, falling action, resolution.'],
 ['structure','jane','Where does this sentence fall in the plot of Jane Eyre?','Near the resolution, when the main conflict has been settled.',['In the exposition, as Jane is introduced.','At the climax, as Rochester proposes.'],'The sentence reports the marriage that settles the central love plot, near the end of the book.'],
 ['narration',null,'What is a first-person narrator?','A narrator who is a character in the story and tells it with “I.”',['A narrator who knows every character’s thoughts.','A narrator who never appears.'],'First-person narration limits us to what the narrator sees and says.'],
 ['narration',null,'What is third-person limited narration?','The narrator stays outside the story and reports one character’s thoughts and view.',['The narrator tells everything using “I.”','The narrator gives every character’s thoughts equally.'],'Third-person limited keeps close to a single mind.'],
 ['narration',null,'What is third-person omniscient narration?','The narrator stands outside the story and can enter the minds of several characters.',['The narrator takes part in the plot.','The narrator knows only one character’s mind.'],'Omniscient means all-knowing.'],
 ['narration','fnar1','Which point of view does this passage use?','First person, limited to what the narrator notices.',['Third-person omniscient.','Second person.'],'“I” tells it, and the narrator learns of the silence only gradually.'],
 ['narration','omni','Which point of view does this passage use?','Third-person omniscient, moving between several minds.',['First person, from Corin’s view.','Second person.'],'The narrator reports what Corin, his mother and the cook each think.'],
 ['narration',null,'What is an unreliable narrator?','A narrator whose account the reader has reason to doubt.',['A narrator who speaks in the third person.','A narrator who is dead at the end.'],'Bias, self-deception or missing knowledge can make a narrator unreliable.'],
 ['narration','unrel','What gives the reader reason to doubt this narrator?','The narrator’s denial is undercut by details the narrator admits.',['The narrator speaks in the present tense.','The narrator uses the word “harbor.”'],'The wet rope and the boat seen by three people weaken the claim.'],
 ['narration','unrel2','Which detail most casts doubt on the narrator’s claim to be patient?','Damage to property that always happens on the days he is angry.',['That he lives on a street.','That people speak of him.'],'The “chance” that fits his anger every time suggests he is the cause.'],
 ['narration',null,'What is a frame narrative?','A story in which one narrative encloses and introduces another.',['A story told without any dialogue.','A story with only one chapter.'],'A frame story tells how another tale came to be told.'],
 ['narration','frame','What structure does this passage set up?','A frame narrative, in which a listener repeats the sailor’s tale.',['A soliloquy by the sailor.','A flashback within a dream.'],'The narrator introduces the sailor’s story and promises to give it as told.'],
 ['narration',null,'Nick Carraway is a narrator and a character in The Great Gatsby. How does this shape what readers learn?','We see Gatsby only through Nick’s observations and judgments, so we weigh his bias.',['We see Gatsby’s private thoughts directly.','Nick has no part in the events.'],'A narrator who takes part has a stake in the story and shows only what he saw or learned.'],
 ['narration','gatsby','What effect does the shift to “we” in the last sentence have?','It widens the reflection from Gatsby and Nick to everyone, including the reader.',['It shows a new narrator has taken over.','It ends the story in the future tense.'],'“We” draws all readers into the image of people struggling against the current.'],
 ['narration','frome','What does the narrator’s remark suggest about the account that follows?','It is pieced together from partial, differing reports, so it is a reconstruction.',['It is an eyewitness account.','It is a legal record.'],'The narrator admits that each teller gave a different version.'],
 ['narration',null,'Frankenstein is told in layers: Walton’s letters, Victor’s account and the creature’s own story. What does letting the creature narrate do?','It gives the creature’s side and complicates the reader’s sympathy.',['It shows Walton wrote the novel as a joke.','It removes the creature’s motives.'],'When the creature speaks, the reader sees Victor’s choices from the other side.'],
 ['narration','frank','Who speaks this line in the novel, and to whom?','Victor Frankenstein, telling his story to Walton, who writes it down for his sister.',['The creature, telling Victor.','Walton, writing to his sister about himself.'],'The line is part of Victor’s account, which Walton records in his letters.'],
 ['narration','jane','What does this sentence show about the narration?','The adult Jane, looking back, speaks directly to the reader.',['A child narrator who does not know the outcome.','A third-person narrator describing Jane.'],'The first-person retrospective narrator addresses “Reader” in her own voice.'],
 ['narration',null,'A play has no narrator. How does an audience learn what a character is thinking?','Through soliloquies, asides and what characters say and do.',['From a printed summary only.','It cannot ever learn anything about thoughts.'],'Drama puts thoughts into speech and action.'],
 ['narration',null,'Why should an essay separate a narrator from the author?','The narrator is a voice the author creates, whose views and gaps may differ from the author’s.',['Authors never write first-person novels.','Narrators always tell the whole truth.'],'Readers analyze the narrator’s choices as part of the work.'],
 ['narration',null,'A first-person narrator recounts a quarrel and always makes himself look blameless. What should a careful reader do?','Ask what he leaves out and compare his account with other evidence in the work.',['Accept everything he says as fact.','Stop reading the work.'],'Weighing a narrator’s account against other details is how readers judge reliability.'],
 ['narration',null,'Late in a novel, a chapter moves from one character’s mind to another’s. What is this called?','A shift in perspective or focalization.',['A change of setting.','A change of title.'],'Shifting perspective changes whose view of events the reader shares.'],
 ['drama',null,'What is a soliloquy?','A speech in which a character alone on stage speaks his or her thoughts aloud.',['A conversation between two characters.','A speech to a crowd that hears it.'],'Soliloquy lets the audience hear private thought.'],
 ['drama',null,'What is an aside?','A brief remark to the audience, or to one other character, that the rest of the characters on stage do not hear.',['A long speech to a large crowd.','A line the author cut.'],'An aside is a quick private remark within a scene.'],
 ['drama',null,'How does a monologue differ from a soliloquy?','A monologue is a long speech heard by other characters; a soliloquy is spoken alone.',['A monologue is shorter than an aside.','They are the same thing.'],'The difference is who hears it.'],
 ['drama',null,'What is dramatic irony?','The audience knows something that one or more characters do not.',['A character says the opposite of what he means.','A sudden reversal at the end.'],'The gap between audience knowledge and character knowledge creates tension or comedy.'],
 ['drama','hamlet1','Which dramatic convention is this?','A soliloquy, in which Hamlet voices his inner conflict.',['An aside to the guards.','A stage direction.'],'Hamlet is alone when he speaks, and the speech weighs enduring suffering against ending it.'],
 ['drama','hamlet3','Hamlet speaks this line as the king and queen talk. Which convention is it?','An aside, a remark heard by the audience but not by the court.',['A soliloquy in an empty room.','A stage direction.'],'The line is quick and private, said while others are present.'],
 ['drama','hamlet2','Which convention is Hamlet using, and what does it reveal?','A soliloquy that reveals his plan to test the king with a play.',['An aside that reveals a joke.','A stage direction that reveals the scenery.'],'He is alone and tells the audience what he intends.'],
 ['drama','sol','Which convention does this scene use?','A soliloquy, because Iris is alone and speaks her thoughts.',['An aside, because she whispers.','A monologue, because others hear her.'],'The stage direction says she is alone on stage.'],
 ['drama','aside','Which convention does Pell use first?','An aside to the audience.',['A soliloquy.','A stage direction.'],'He speaks quietly to the audience while the Duke does not hear.'],
 ['drama','aside','What does the contrast between Pell’s two lines show?','He says one thing to the audience and another to the Duke, revealing his deceit.',['He tells the Duke the truth both times.','He is speaking only to himself.'],'The aside shows what he really thinks while his spoken words flatter.'],
 ['drama','irony','Why does Gil’s line create dramatic irony?','The audience has seen Lena hide the ring, while Gil does not know.',['Gil is speaking to the audience.','Gil is lying to the Mayor on purpose.'],'The audience knows more than Gil, and his praise lands in a comic or painful way.'],
 ['drama','voss','Is Captain Voss’s speech a soliloquy?','No; he speaks to the guards, who hear him.',['Yes; he is alone on stage.','Yes; he speaks to the audience only.'],'A soliloquy is spoken alone, and the guards are on stage listening.'],
 ['drama','caesar2','Antony speaks these lines on the stage in front of a crowd. Why is this not a soliloquy?','The Roman crowd hears it; it is addressed to them.',['He is speaking to the audience only.','He is speaking silently.'],'A speech to listeners on stage is a speech, not a soliloquy.'],
 ['drama','dream2','Why does the lovers’ confusion in A Midsummer Night’s Dream produce dramatic irony?','The audience knows about the love potion and its effects, while the lovers do not.',['The audience is as confused as the lovers.','Puck also does not know about the potion.'],'The audience watches the lovers misread a situation that the audience understands.'],
 ['drama','dream3','Puck speaks to the audience at the end. What does this convention do?','It breaks the illusion and invites the audience’s forgiveness and applause.',['It continues a hidden aside.','It starts a new subplot.'],'The epilogue turns to the audience directly and suggests the play was a dream.'],
 ['drama','earnest','What comic technique does Lady Bracknell’s sentence use?','A reversal of expectation: it treats a tragic loss as a social fault.',['A rhyme that ends the play.','A stage direction.'],'Calling the loss of both parents “carelessness” is Wilde’s satire of Victorian manners.'],
 ['drama','caesar1','What does Caesar’s question suggest about his feelings?','He is shocked that a trusted friend like Brutus has joined the attack.',['He is calling for his guards.','He does not recognize anyone.'],'The words show that Brutus’s betrayal wounds him; readers usually read it as shock.'],
 ['drama',null,'What are stage directions?','Notes in a script about actions, settings and delivery that are not spoken.',['Lines spoken only by the narrator.','The title of each act.'],'They tell the cast and readers what happens and how.'],
 ['drama',null,'How are plays usually divided?','Into acts, each made up of scenes.',['Into chapters and verses.','Into stanzas and couplets.'],'Acts mark large movements, and scenes mark changes of place or time.'],
 ['drama',null,'What is blank verse?','Unrhymed iambic pentameter.',['Rhymed couplets.','Prose without meter.'],'Shakespeare uses blank verse often, mixing it with prose and rhyme.'],
 ['drama',null,'What is a tragic hero?','A main character whose downfall comes from a flaw or error, often bringing awareness too late.',['A character who always wins.','A character who is the narrator.'],'Tragedy follows a person of stature to a fall tied to a mistake or flaw.'],
 ['drama',null,'What is comic relief?','A funny moment that eases the tension in a serious work.',['The final scene of a comedy.','A summary of a tragedy.'],'Comic relief provides a break and can also comment on the main action.'],
 ['argument',null,'On the AP Literature exam, what does the third free-response question ask?','A literary argument about a novel or play of the student’s choice that fits the prompt.',['An analysis of a poem printed on the exam.','A summary of a passage of nonfiction.'],'Question 3 gives a concept and asks for an essay on a work the student selects.'],
 ['argument','jane','Which sentence embeds the quotation most smoothly?','Brontë ends Jane’s story with the plain declaration “Reader, I married him,” which shows Jane in charge of her own account.',['Reader, I married him. Brontë wrote this.','The quotation is “Reader, I married him.”'],'A smooth embed fits the words into the writer’s own sentence and adds an interpretation.'],
 ['argument',null,'Which thesis about The Great Gatsby is defensible and about the text?','By showing Gatsby only through Nick’s eyes, the novel makes the reader weigh a dream against a narrator’s judgment of it.',['The Great Gatsby is a famous American novel.','Gatsby is a man with many parties.'],'A thesis takes a position about how the work’s choices create meaning.'],
 ['argument',null,'Which thesis about Frankenstein is defensible and about the text?','By letting the creature tell his own story, Shelley invites sympathy that makes Victor’s neglect harder to excuse.',['Frankenstein is a book about a scientist.','Shelley wrote Frankenstein in 1818.'],'The first choice makes an argument that a reader could dispute and the text could answer.'],
 ['argument',null,'Which thesis about A Doll’s House is strongest?','Ibsen’s single living room turns Nora’s marriage into a performance, so her leaving is a step out of a role.',['A Doll’s House is set at Christmas.','Ibsen was a Norwegian playwright.'],'A strong thesis names the setting choice and what it means.'],
 ['argument',null,'Which statement is plot summary rather than a thesis?','Hamlet sees his father’s ghost, stages a play, and later fights Laertes.',['Hamlet’s delay is shaped by his habit of testing every belief.','Hamlet’s soliloquies make the audience a partner in his doubt.'],'A summary retells events; a thesis argues something about them.'],
 ['argument',null,'What is the best way to use evidence from a long work?','Choose specific scenes and details from across the work that support the claim.',['Quote a whole chapter without explanation.','Use only the first scene.'],'Specific details from different parts of the work show a pattern.'],
 ['argument',null,'Which sentence is commentary?','The repeated image of the clock shows that time has stopped for Ilse and gives the scene its weight.',['Ilse puts the letter on the mantel.','The scene begins in the morning.'],'Commentary explains what the details do; the other lines only report them.'],
 ['argument',null,'On Question 3, how should a student choose a work?','Pick one known well enough to cite accurate scenes, details and characters.',['Pick the longest work available.','Pick one never read, to avoid bias.'],'Specific, accurate references are the heart of a strong argument.'],
 ['argument',null,'On a literary argument essay, what kind of reference to the work earns credit for evidence?','Specific, accurate references to scenes or details, with direct quotation helpful but not required.',['Only quotations of a full page.','Only the title and the author’s name.'],'The rubric looks for specific, relevant evidence; accurate paraphrase can serve.'],
 ['argument','salt','Which thesis for this fictional novel names a choice and its effect?','By having Ines return to find her village changed, the novel shows that success can cost a person her sense of home.',['Ines’s story is about salt.','The Lantern Ferry has a heroine named Ines.'],'The first choice argues what the plot does and what it shows.'],
 ['argument',null,'Which approach can help earn the sophistication point?','Explaining a tension or complexity in the work and how it shapes the argument.',['Adding more plot summary.','Using long words throughout.'],'Sophistication rewards a deeper grasp of tension, context or complexity; it is not length or vocabulary.'],
 ['argument',null,'How does a topic sentence differ from a thesis?','It states the single claim of one paragraph that supports the thesis.',['It states the claim of the whole essay.','It is a quotation from the work.'],'The thesis is the essay’s claim and each topic sentence is a step toward it.'],
 ['argument',null,'A student writes, “Hamlet has soliloquies.” How could it be improved?','Say what a soliloquy shows, quote briefly, and explain how it supports the argument.',['Add more names of plays.','Delete the sentence.'],'Naming a device earns little; showing what it does with evidence earns credit.'],
 ['argument','hamlet1','Which sentence embeds the Hamlet quotation best?','Hamlet’s opening, “To be, or not to be, that is the question,” turns a private doubt into a choice about whether to endure suffering.',['Hamlet says To be, or not to be, that is the question.','Quote: “To be, or not to be, that is the question.”'],'The best sentence fits the words into the writer’s grammar and says what they do.'],
 ['argument',null,'Which concluding sentence goes beyond restating the thesis?','The play suggests that delay itself can be a form of action, and so asks what we owe to certainty before we act.',['In conclusion, this essay has argued many things.','Thank you for reading about Hamlet.'],'A strong conclusion points to a larger meaning the argument has uncovered.'],
 ['argument',null,'Which claim traces change across a whole work?','Over the course of the play, Nora moves from playing a role to speaking for herself.',['Nora appears in the first scene.','Nora speaks in Act 1.'],'A claim about change covers the whole span from beginning to end.'],
 ['argument',null,'Which opening sentence includes a concession in a paragraph about Gatsby?','Although Gatsby’s wealth seems to prove his dream real, the novel shows what the dream costs.',['Gatsby is rich.','Gatsby gives parties.'],'A concession grants a point and then argues past it.']];
const TRY=['Fictional passage: “At the start, Pia hid behind her brother whenever a stranger spoke. By the end, she faced the whole council and read out the petition herself.” What kind of character is Pia? A: A dynamic character, because she changes, B: A static character, because she stays the same, C: A foil, because she tells the story. Answer A, B or C.',
 'Fictional passage: “Mina set the cup down. Twenty years earlier, in her mother’s kitchen, the same cup had rattled on its saucer the night the telegram came. She picked it up again and steadied her hand.” Which technique is used? A: Flashback, B: Soliloquy, C: Frame narrative. Answer A, B or C.',
 'Fictional scene: “(Ben tucks a winning ticket into his boot. Mary enters, tearful.) MARY: Ben, we have lost everything; we cannot buy bread. BEN: Then we must be brave.” Why is this dramatic irony? A: The audience knows about the ticket, but Mary does not, B: Ben speaks to the audience alone, C: Mary narrates the story. Answer A, B or C.'];
const tryIt=s=>`Try it: ${s[0].toLowerCase()+s.slice(1)}`;
export default {
id:'language-h-ap-lit-longer-fiction-and-drama',subject:'language',level:5,grade:9,standard:'AP English Literature and Composition Course and Exam Description, Unit 3 Big Ideas CHR, SET, STR, NAR and LAN',
title:'Longer Fiction or Drama I',
goal:'Read novels and plays at AP depth: see how characters are built and set against foils, how setting and symbols carry meaning, how plot structure, subplots and flashback shape a story, how a narrator’s position and reliability limit what we know, how dramatic conventions such as the soliloquy, the aside and dramatic irony work on stage, and how to write a defensible thesis about a longer work.',
points:20,
skills:{character:'Characters, foils and change',setting:'Setting, mood and symbols',structure:'Plot structure, subplots and flashback',narration:'Narrator and perspective',drama:'Dramatic conventions',argument:'A defensible claim about a longer work'},
outline:[['CHR','Characters: foils, complexity, and change across a longer work',['character']],['SET','Setting: how place, time, mood and symbols carry meaning',['setting']],['STR','Plot structure: exposition, conflict, climax, resolution, subplots and flashback',['structure']],['NAR','Narration: narrator, perspective and reliability in longer works',['narration']],['STR','Dramatic conventions: soliloquy, aside, dramatic irony and stage form',['drama']],['LAN','Literary argumentation: a defensible thesis about a longer work',['argument']]],
steps:[
 {skill:'character',say:'A foil is a character whose contrasting traits sharpen our sense of another character. Setting two people side by side makes each one clearer. In this fictional pair, Mara counts the loaves twice while Dev trusts the numbers to fix themselves, so her order and his ease define each other. Always name the trait that contrasts, then say what the contrast helps us see.',pic:pass(FP,P('foil')[1],false)},
 {skill:'character',say:'Longer works give characters room to change. A dynamic character is altered by events, while a static character stays the same even when much happens around him. A flat character has one or two traits, and a round character is complex and can surprise us. To argue about change, compare a character early in the work with the same character late, and point to what caused the difference.',pic:tab('Kinds of characters',[['dynamic','changes in a significant way'],['static','stays essentially the same'],['flat','few traits, predictable'],['round','complex, can surprise us']],['Meaning']),
  check:{prompt:tryIt(TRY[0]),answer:'A',options:['A','B','C'],hint:'Compare Pia at the start with Pia at the end. Does she stay the same or change?',pic:pass(FP,['At the start, Pia hid behind her brother whenever a stranger spoke.','By the end, she faced the whole council and read out the petition herself.'])}},
 {skill:'character',say:'Shakespeare sets several foils around Hamlet, a prince who is told to avenge his murdered father and keeps delaying. Laertes acts fast when his own father is killed. Fortinbras, a prince of Norway, leads an army across Denmark to win a small piece of land for honor. Their quick action sets off Hamlet’s hesitation, and Hamlet himself notices the contrast.',pic:tab('Foils to Hamlet',[['Hamlet','thinks, tests and delays'],['Laertes','acts fast to avenge his father'],['Fortinbras','leads an army for honor']],['Trait'])},
 {skill:'setting',say:'Setting is the time and place of a work, and often its social world. Details of setting build mood, the feeling a reader gets. This fictional house has a green door and faces east, which tell us little, but weeds through the porch boards and unswept steps tell us a great deal. Pick the details that carry mood, and say what feeling they create.',pic:pass(FP,P('house')[1],false)},
 {skill:'setting',say:'Settings can also carry meaning as symbols. In The Great Gatsby, the green light at the end of Daisy’s dock is commonly read as Gatsby’s longing, and the gray valley of ashes as the decay beneath the glitter of the wealthy. These are common readings, and a good essay supports them with details. Look also for contrasts of place, such as new-money West Egg and old-money East Egg.',pic:tab('Gatsby’s settings, commonly read',[['green light','Gatsby’s longing for Daisy'],['valley of ashes','decay beneath the glitter'],['West Egg and East Egg','new money and old money']],['Common reading'])},
 {skill:'structure',say:'Plot has a familiar shape. Exposition introduces the characters and situation, rising action builds the conflict through complications, the climax is the turning point of highest tension, falling action follows it, and the resolution settles the outcome. Real works bend the shape, so use it as a map, not a rule. In Julius Caesar, readers often place the turning point at the assassination in Act 3.',pic:arc('The shape of a plot',[[2,6],[22,10],[40,17],[55,27],[70,18],[84,10],[98,6]],[[8,2,'Exposition'],[30,8,'Rising action'],[55,31,'Climax'],[80,22,'Falling action'],[92,2,'Resolution']])},
 {skill:'structure',say:'A subplot is a second storyline that runs alongside the main plot and often echoes or tests it. In Hamlet, the stories of Laertes and Fortinbras set other young men beside the prince. A flashback, by contrast, takes us to an earlier time, and foreshadowing hints at what will come. Ask what each device adds that the main plot alone could not.',pic:flow('Plot tools and what they add',[['Subplot','Echoes the main plot'],['Flashback','Shows the past'],['Foreshadowing','Hints ahead']]),
  check:{prompt:tryIt(TRY[1]),answer:'A',options:['A','B','C'],hint:'The passage jumps back twenty years to a memory. What is that called?',pic:pass(FP,['Mina set the cup down.','Twenty years earlier, in her mother’s kitchen, the same cup had rattled on its saucer the night the telegram came.','She picked it up again and steadied her hand.'])}},
 {skill:'structure',say:'Structure can reshape meaning. A Doll’s House (1879) keeps nearly all its action in one living room, and its turning point comes when Torvald reads Krogstad’s letter and shows his anger, so that Nora sees her marriage clearly. Her leaving is the resolution. In Ethan Frome, the story opens in the present and returns to the past, so the ending shows what the years have cost.',pic:flow('A Doll’s House: a turning point',[['Secret','Nora has hidden a loan'],['Letter','Torvald reacts in anger'],['Choice','Nora decides to leave']])},
 {skill:'narration',say:'Narration decides what a reader can know. A first-person narrator is a character who says “I,” and sees only what he or she sees. Third-person limited stays close to one mind, and third-person omniscient can move among many. In the fictional passage, the narrator learns that a sister has stopped speaking only when the table falls quiet, which is what first-person narration allows.',pic:pass(FP,P('fnar1')[1],false)},
 {skill:'narration',say:'A frame narrative places one story inside another. Frankenstein (1818) opens with letters from the explorer Walton, who records Victor’s account, which in turn holds the creature’s own story. Ethan Frome opens with a narrator who has pieced the tale together from several people. Each layer filters what we learn, so ask who is telling, to whom, and what that teller might gain or leave out.',pic:sketch('Frankenstein: layers of narration',[['',[[0,2],[100,2],[100,32],[0,32]],0],['',[[12,6],[88,6],[88,27],[12,27]],1],['',[[26,10],[74,10],[74,21],[26,21]],2]],[[50,29.5,'Walton’s letters'],[50,24,'Victor’s account'],[50,15.5,'The creature’s story']])},
 {skill:'narration',say:'Sometimes a narrator cannot be fully trusted. An unreliable narrator may be biased, self-deceived or missing facts, and the reader notices gaps between what is said and what the details show. In this fictional passage the speaker denies being at the harbor, yet admits his dock rope is still wet. When a narrator protests too much, weigh the claim against other evidence.',pic:pass(FP,P('unrel')[1],false)},
 {skill:'drama',say:'A play has no narrator, so conventions give the audience access to thought. In a soliloquy a character alone on stage speaks his or her thoughts aloud, as Hamlet does in “To be, or not to be.” An aside is a brief remark the audience hears and others on stage do not, as when Hamlet mutters that he is more than kin and less than kind. A monologue, by contrast, is heard by other characters.',pic:pass('Hamlet (c. 1600), Act 1, Scene 2: an aside',P('hamlet3')[1],false)},
 {skill:'drama',say:'Dramatic irony arises when the audience knows something that a character does not. In A Midsummer Night’s Dream, the audience knows that Puck’s love potion is behind the lovers’ muddles, while the lovers do not, and Puck laughs at the mortals’ confusion. Irony turns watching into a pleasure of knowing more, and sometimes into suspense or pity.',pic:flow('Dramatic irony in A Midsummer Night’s Dream',[['Audience knows','The potion causes the muddle'],['Lovers do not','They blame one another'],['Effect','Comedy of mistaken love']]),
  check:{prompt:tryIt(TRY[2]),answer:'A',options:['A','B','C'],hint:'Ask who knows about the ticket in the boot, and who does not.',pic:pass(FS,['(Ben tucks a winning ticket into his boot. Mary enters, tearful.)','MARY: Ben, we have lost everything; we cannot buy bread.','BEN: Then we must be brave.'])}},
 {skill:'argument',say:'A thesis about a longer work makes a claim that the whole text can support and a reader could dispute. It names a choice the author makes and what that choice does. A weak thesis only names the work, the author or a device. Test yours by asking whether it is arguable, whether it is specific to this work, and whether you could prove it with scenes from beginning to end.',pic:board('Weak and strong theses about a longer work',['Weak: Gatsby is a famous novel','Weak: Hamlet has soliloquies','Strong: Nick’s view makes us weigh the dream','Test: arguable, specific, provable'],['a plain fact','a feature, with no claim','arguable, from the text','the check'])},
 {skill:'argument',say:'The AP Literature exam asks for three essays. Question 3 asks for a literary argument about a novel or play you choose, in answer to a prompt that names an idea such as a character, a setting or a conflict. The rubric gives up to six points: one for the thesis, up to four for evidence and commentary, and one for sophistication. Pick a work you know well enough to cite accurate scenes.',pic:tab('How a literary argument is scored',[['Thesis','1 point'],['Evidence and commentary','up to 4 points'],['Sophistication','1 point']],['Points'])},
 {skill:'argument',summary:true,say:'Pull the unit together with one habit: read a longer work for how its choices make meaning. Ask who the characters are set against, how the place and symbols work, how the plot is built, who tells the story and what that teller leaves out, and what the conventions of the stage reveal. Then write a thesis you can prove with evidence from across the work, and explain each piece in your own commentary.',pic:flow('Longer works: the habit',[['Notice','Character, setting, plot, voice'],['Interpret','What the choices do'],['Argue','Thesis, evidence, commentary']])}],
progression:{requires:['language-h-ap-lit-poetry'],operations:2,demand:'Read longer fiction and drama at AP depth: characters and foils, setting and symbols, plot structure, narrator and perspective, dramatic conventions, and a defensible thesis proved with evidence and commentary.'},
items(add){
 const seen=new Set(TRY);
 const put=(skill,prompt,answer,pic,explanation,accept)=>{if(seen.has(prompt))return;seen.add(prompt);add(skill,prompt,answer,pic,explanation,accept);};
 ROWS.forEach(([skill,key,q,right,wrong,why],k)=>{const c=choose(right,wrong,k);
  if(!key){put(skill,`${q} ${c.text}. ${ask(c.n)}`,c.letter,null/* 25 Oct: no picture; it only repeated the question in tiny type */,why,[right,dot(right)]);return;}
  if(key==='salt'){put(skill,`${SALT} ${q} ${c.text}. ${ask(c.n)}`,c.letter,pass(FP,[SALT]),why,[right,dot(right)]);return;}
  const [src,lines]=P(key),text=lines.join(' / ');
  put(skill,`${src}: “${text}” ${q} ${c.text}. ${ask(c.n)}`,c.letter,pass(src,lines),why,[right,dot(right)]);});
}};
